<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/advanced/psycholinguistics-and-acquisition">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Psycholinguistics studies <strong>language in the mind and in real time</strong>: how children
    acquire grammar from imperfect input, how adults parse sentences millisecond by millisecond,
    and what bilingual minds reveal about the system. It is where linguistics meets the
    psychology and neuroscience ladders.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="acquisition, the innateness debate, real-time parsing, bilingual minds" widget="equationStep" config={psylingCfg} />

  <h2>The evidence base</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The wug test and over-regularisation:</strong> children say "goed" AFTER saying "went" correctly — the U-shaped learning curve proves rule extraction. The strongest evidence for internal grammar.</li>
    <li><strong>Creole languages as the natural experiment:</strong> children of pidgin speakers turned crude contact languages into full grammars in one generation (Bickerton) — grammar created from scratch by children, supporting built-in biases.</li>
    <li><strong>Sign languages are full languages:</strong> deaf children acquire sign on the same schedule as speech — the language faculty is modality-independent, one of the field's deepest findings.</li>
    <li><strong>Animal communication contrast:</strong> trained apes learn symbols but show no recursion or productivity — the contrast that keeps defining what makes human language special (Hockett's design features).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Berko (1958); Bickerton, Roots of Language (1981); Crain & Nakayama (1987); Trueswell et al. on parsing.</p>
</TopicPage>
